      "content": "While temperature control is critical, sudden bloom can also stem from rapid condensation when the batch slides directly from warm to cold air, causing moisture or unstable fats to migrate before you can complete the final cooling stage. This instability often happens if your work environment is cooler than the chocolate (below 18°C/64°F), forcing the liquid mixture to solidify too quickly without proper crystal refinement. To prevent this in future batches, always ensure you achieve \"melting\" conditions within the bowl first, allow the chocolate to cool below its working temperature while moving away from fresh airflow, and maintain your workspace temperature above 20°C to avoid shocking the chocolate during the final proofing stage.",
